 اعمال هيجل

 The standard German edition of Hegel’s collected works is the twentyvolume Jubilee Edition, edited by H. Glockner and published in Stuttgart from  to . For the Englishspeaking reader wondering where to begin reading Hegel’s own works, I would recommend following the order of the works discussed in this book. English translations of these works are:

 Lectures on the Philosophy of History,

 tr. J. Sibree (Dover, New York, ). The introduction to these lectures is available separately, as

 Reason in History: A General Introduction to the Philosophy of History , tr. R. S. Hartman (Library of Liberal Arts, New York, ), and as

 Lectures on the Philosophy of World History. Introduction: Reason in History , tr. H. B. Nisbet (Cambridge University
Press, Cambridge, ). The latter edition contains additional scholarly material.

 Hegel’s Philosophy of Right,

 tr. T. M. Knox (Oxford University Press, London, ).

 The Phenomenology of Mind,

 tr. J. B. Baillie (Harper & Row, New York, ). There is also a more recent and, in the view of many, more reliable translation by A. V. Miller, entitled

 Hegel’s Phenomenology of Spirit (Oxford University Press, Oxford, ).

 Hegel’s Science of Logic,

 tr. W. H. Johnston and L. G. Struthers in two volumes (Allen & Unwin, London, ); also more recently translated by A. V. Miller (Allen & Unwin, London, ).

Probably the most important of Hegel’s other works is his

 Encyclopedia of the Philosophical Sciences . This work has been published in English in its separate components. The section on logic, sometimes called the 'Lesser Logic’, was translated by W. Wallace and published under the confusing title

 The Logic of Hegel (Clarendon Press, Oxford, ). There is another edition, with a foreword by J. N. Findlay (Clarendon Press, Oxford, ). The second part is available as

 Hegel’s Philosophy of Nature , tr. A. V. Miller (Clarendon Press, Oxford, ) and also in a translation by M. J. Petry (Allen & Unwin, London, ). Wallace’s original translation of

 Hegel’s Philosophy of Mind (Clarendon Press, Oxford, ) has been republished, together with additional material translated by A. V. Miller (Clarendon Press, Oxford, ).

 English translations of Hegel’s other writings are:

 Early Theological Writings,

 tr. T. M. Knox (University of Chicago
Press, Chicago, ). This volume contains the earliest of Hegel’s surviving works.

 Hegel’s Political Writings,

 tr. T. M. Knox (Clarendon Press, Oxford, ). Hegel’s occasional political essays, such as that on the English Reform Bill, are collected here.

 Lectures on the Philosophy of Religion,

 tr. E. B. Speirs and J. B. Sanderson, in three volumes (Routledge & Kegan Paul, London, ).

 Hegel’s Aesthetics,

 tr. T. M. Knox, in two volumes (Clarendon Press, Oxford, ).

 Lectures on the History of
Philosophy,

 tr. E. S. Haldane and F. H. Simson, in three volumes (Routledge & Kegan Paul, London, ).

 For a convenient selection of Hegel’s writings, with an extensive bibliography, see

 The Hegel Reader

 by Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel, edited by Stephen Houlgate (Blackwell, Oxford, ).

 مءلفات عن هيجل

 For readers wishing for further guidance, I would recommend Richard Norman’s slim and stimulating volume,

 Hegel’s
Phenomenology: A Philosophical Introduction (Sussex University
Press, Brighton, ). Next could come Ivan Soll,

 An Introduction to Hegel’s Metaphysics (University of Chicago Press, Chicago, ). After that one might move on to the shorter of Charles Taylor’s two books on Hegel,

 Hegel and Modern Society (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, ), or, if one is ready for it, to the much longer and more difficult

 Hegel (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, ). For a detailed study of Hegel’s

Phenomenology , see Robert Solomon,

 In the Spirit of Hegel: A Study of Hegel’s
Phenomenology (Oxford University Press, New York, ); H. S. Harris,

 Hegel’s Ladder: A Commentary on Hegel’s
Phenomenology of Spirit (Hackett, Indianapolis, ); and Michael N. Forster,

 Hegel’s Idea of a Phenomenology of Spirit (University of Chicago Press, Chicago, ).

 On Hegel’s life, see

 Hegel: A Biography

 by Terry P. Pinkard (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, ). The two volumes

 Hegel’s Development: Towards the Sunlight, 

 and

 Hegel’s Development: Night Thoughts (Jena )

 by H. S. Harris (Clarendon Press, Oxford, , ) is a fine study of Hegel’s early years. The influential work by George Lukacs,

 The Young Hegel,

 is available in an English translation by R. Livingstone (Merlin Press, London, ).

 Tom Rockmore puts Hegel’s theory of knowledge into its historical context in

 Before and After Hegel: A Historical Introduction to Hegel’s Thought (University of California, Berkeley and Los Angeles, ). Older, but still useful works are: Edward Caird,

 Hegel (Blackwood, Edinburgh, ); Benedetto Croce,

 What is Living and What is Dead in the
Philosophy of Hegel?,

 tr. D. Ainslie (Russell & Russell, New York, ); W. T. Stace,

 The Philosophy of Hegel (Dover, New York, ); and J. N. Findlay,

 Hegel: A Reexamination (Allen & Unwin, London, ).

 Among notable books on Hegel’s social and political ideas are: Herbert Marcuse,

 Reason and Revolution (Humanities Press, New York, ); Shlomo Avineri,

 Hegel’s Theory of the Modern State (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, ); Raymond Plant,

 Hegel (Allen & Unwin, London, ); and Judith Shklar,

 Freedom and Independence: A Study of the Political Ideas of Hegel’s 'Phenomenology of Mind’ (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, ). Some heated exchanges over the extent to which Hegel’s philosophy supports the authoritarian state have been collected by Walter Kaufmann in a volume entitled

 Hegel’s Political Philosophy (Atherton
Press, New York, ). There is also a collection of articles edited by Z.
Pelczynski, entitled

 Hegel’s Political
Philosophy (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, ). Alan
Patten’s

 Hegel’s Idea of Freedom (Oxford University Press, New York, ) gives an accessible account of Hegel’s social and political philosophy. Together with this one might readthough with considerable cautionKarl Popper’s provocative attempt to find the origins of totalitarianism in Hegel’s thought: see

 The Open Society and Its Enemies , vol. II, chapter  (Routledge & Kegan
Paul, London, ).

 For further study of the religious aspect of Hegel’s thought, see Emil Fackenheim,

 The Religious Dimension of Hegel’s Thought (Indiana University Press, Bloomington, ), and Bernard Reardon,

 Hegel’s Philosophy of Religion (Macmillan, London, ). See also Robert C. Whittemore’s article, 'Hegel as Panentheist’ in

 Tulane Studies in Philosophy , vol. IX (), pp. . This volume is a special issue on Hegel.

 There are innumerable scholarly articles on Hegel in the philosophical journals. Some of the best have been collected in Alasdair MacIntyre (ed.),

 Hegel: A Collection of Critical Essays (Anchor, New York, ).

 The Cambridge Companion to Hegel,

 edited by Frederick C. Beiser (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, ) is useful for the serious student of Hegel.

 Finally, the various transformations of Hegel’s thought by the Young Hegelians are well described in Sidney Hook,

 From Hegel to Marx (Humanities Press, New York, ), and also in David McLellan,

 The Young Hegelians and Karl Marx (Macmillan, London, ).





